aluating the same $(x,C)$ alone. This separates algorithmic exactness from finite-precision effects caused by physical batch shape.\nL390: #### Position selection.\nL391: \nL392: The target chooses\nL393: \nL394:  | $$s^{\\star}(x)=\\argmax_{s\\in\\mathcal{M}(x)}\\max_{v\\in\\mathcal{V}}p_{\\theta,s}(v\\mid x,C).$$  |\nL395: \nL396: All argmax operations use a fixed deterministic tie-breaking rule. In particular, $s^{\\star}(x)$ depends only on the current target state and not on sampling randomness that has not yet been consumed.\nL397: \nL398: For $s\\in\\mathcal{M}(x)$ and $v\\in\\mathcal{V}$, write\nL399: \nL400:  | $$x\\oplus(s,v)$$  |\nL401: \nL402: for the state obtained by replacing $x_{s}=\\textsc{m}$ by $v$.\nL403: #### Greedy transition.\nL404: \nL405: At $T=0$,\nL406: \nL407:  | $$v^{\\star}_{0}(x)=\\argmax_{v}p_{\\theta,s^{\\star}(x)}(v\\mid x,C),$$  |\nL408: \nL409: and therefore\nL410: \nL411:  | $$\\Phi_{0}:\\mathcal{X}_{\\mathrm{nt}}\\rightarrow\\mathcal{X},\\qquad\\Phi_{0}(x)=x\\oplus\\bigl(s^{\\star}(x),v^{\\star}_{0}(x)\\bigr).$$  |\nL412: \nL413: Starting from initial block state $x_{0}$, draft-free greedy decoding is the deterministic orbit\nL414: \nL415:  | $$x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{0}=x_{0},\\qquad x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{j+1}=\\Phi_{0}(x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{j})$$  |\nL416: \nL417: until a terminal state is reached.\nL418: #### Sampling transition.\nL419: \nL420: At $T>0$, the target token law at position $s$ is\nL421: \nL422:  | $$\\pi_{T,s}(v\\mid x,C)=\\frac{p_{\\theta,s}(v\\mid x,C)^{1/T}}{\\sum_{u\\in\\mathcal{V}}p_{\\theta,s}(u\\mid x,C)^{1/T}}.$$  |\nL423: \nL424: Thus standard draft-free target sampling at state $x$ first chooses the deterministic position $s^{\\star}(x)$ and then draws\nL425: \nL426:  | $$V\\sim\\pi_{T,s^{\\star}(x)}(\\cdot\\mid x,C).$$  |\nL427: #### Shared Gumbel representation.\nL428: \nL429: Let $a(s)$ denote the absolute output position corresponding to local block position $s$. Draw\nL430: \nL431:  | $$G_{a,v}\\overset{\\mathrm{iid}}{\\sim}\\mathrm{Gumbel}(0,1)$$  |\nL432: \nL433: for every absolute position $a$ and token $v$, and let\nL434: \nL435:  | $$G=\\{G_{a,v}\\}_{a,v}.$$  |\nL436: \nL437: For fixed $G$, define\nL438: \nL439:  | $$v^{\\star}_{G}(x)=\\argmax_{v}\\left[\\frac{1}{T}\\log p_{\\theta,s^{\\star}(x)}(v\\mid x,C)+G_{a(s^{\\star}(x)),v}\\right]$$  |\nL440: \nL441: and\nL990: SPOT (ours)  | 79.1 (2.58)  | 62.0 (2.57)  | 57.5 (3.49)  | 40.7 (3.26)  | 67.1 (3.10)  | 48.9 (3.16)  | 88.9 (2.30)  | 72.0 (2.29)\nL991: SPOT-$\\gamma$ (ours)  | 79.8 (2.60)  | 66.2 (2.93)  | 58.4 (3.56)  | 43.4 (4.23)  | 67.5 (3.28)  | 51.7 (3.95)  | 90.1 (2.40)  | 76.7 (2.67)\nL992: Spiffy graph  | 98.7 (2.31)  | 82.3 (2.57)  | 86.5 (2.78)  | 66.6 (2.62)  | 91.7 (2.70)  | 72.4 (3.07)  | 104.5 (2.40)  | 90.0 (2.52)\nL993: free chain, match  | 99.0 (2.32)  | 92.6 (2.29)  | 93.5 (2.56)  | 78.7 (2.14)  | 92.6 (2.69)  | 84.6 (2.64)  | 104.8 (2.36)  | 99.0 (2.25)\nL994: free chain, RS  | 99.0 (1.43)  | 96.2 (1.23)  | 71.4 (2.05)  | 67.6 (1.60)  | 87.8 (1.72)  | 83.5 (1.49)  | 115.8 (1.26)  | 115.4 (1.06)\nL995: BlockSpec tree  | 141.4 (1.55)  | 121.2 (1.53)  | 137.5 (1.68)  | 114.3 (1.36)  | 138.2 (1.73)  | 116.0 (1.72)  | 141.5 (1.68)  | 122.4 (1.62)\nL996: #### The smallest target is where padding costs most.\nL997: SDAR-1.7B is the setting in the benchmark where baselines most often take a throughput column from SPOT: Spiffy is ahead by $0.01$–$0.14$ on GSM8K at $D20$ and on MATH500 at both budgets, the three settings with the lowest acceptance. The NFE ordering is untouched – SPOT drafts $14$–$23\\%$ fewer forwards than Spiffy in those same cells – so the gap is verification cost, not drafting: a $1.7$B forward is cheap enough that the $(1{+}D)L$ padded positions are a large share of it.\nL998: SPOT-$\\gamma$, which drops the padding, recovers all but one of those columns at $T{=}0$ and all of them at $T{=}1$.\nL999: Table 12: Main results, LLaDA2.0-mini b32 (MoE, exact prefix-KV cache), $T{=}0$, $n{