71:     1. cite61†C.1 PI Utility L72:     2. cite62†C.2 Sampled-token Reverse KL L73:     3. cite63†C.3 Error-step KL Contrast L74:   13. cite64†D How Information Leakage Distorts PI Utility L75:     1. cite65†ALFWorld: the model copies the goal-completing action. L76:     2. cite66†$\\tau^{2}$-retail: the 4B thinking student reports a result instead of producing it. L77:     3. cite67†Implication. L78:   14. cite68†E PI Ablations L79:     1. cite69†All three metrics flag mismatched and other-domain PI. L80:     2. cite70†A wrong PI can still produce a large shift. L81:     3. cite71†PI length is not the main driver of the KL metrics. L82:   15. cite72†F Per-Task PI Selection L83:     1. cite73†Pools. L84:     2. cite74†Scoring. L85:     3. cite75†Training. L86:   16. cite76†G Additional Results L87:     1. cite77†G.1 OPSD Performance L88:     2. cite78†G.2 Offline Metrics L89:   17. cite79†H Prompts and Example Hints L90:     1. cite80†H.1 Agent Prompts L91:       1. cite81†ALFWorld. L92:       2. cite82†AppWorld. L93:       3. cite83†$\\tau^{2}$-retail. L94:       4. cite84†Teacher context. L95:     2. cite85†H.2 Hint-Generation Prompts L96:       1. cite86†H.2.1 Summary L97:       2. cite87†H.2.2 Skill L98:       3. cite88†H.2.3 Checklist L99:     3. cite89†H.3 Example Hints L100:       1. cite90†ALFWorld. L101:       2. cite91†AppWorld. L102:       3. cite92†$\\tau^{2}$-retail. L103: cite93†License: CC BY-SA 4.0†info.arxiv.org L104: \nL105: arXiv:2610.22512v1 [cs.LG] 15 Oct 2026\nL106: \nL107: \\papertype\nL108: \nL109: Scale AI Research \\contact Code and data: cite94†https://github.com/zkjzou/PI-OPSD†github.com L110: \nL111: ^{†}^{†}footnotetext: ^{∗}Corresponding authors: zkjzou@umich.edu, anas.mahmoud@scale.com.^{†}^{†}footnotetext: ^{†}Work done during an internship at Scale AI.\nL112: # Predicting Privileged Information Effectiveness for On-Policy Self-Distillation\nL113: \nL114: Kaijian Zou Affiliation: University of Michigan – Ann Arbor    Emiliano Penaloza Affiliation: Mila    MohammadHossein Rezaei Affiliation: Scale AI    Anisha Gunjal Affiliation: Scale AI    Mohit Raghavendra Affiliation: Scale AI    Lu Wang Affiliation: University of Michigan – Ann Arbor    Yunzhong He Affiliation: Scale AI    Anas Mahmoud Affiliation: Scale AI\nL115: ###### Abstract\nL116: On-policy self-distillation (OPSD) enables large language models (LLMs) to learn from privileged information (PI) without a separate teacher model. However, prior work largely treats PI as a fixed design choice, leaving the effects of its content and construction poorly understood. This challenge is particularly important in multi-turn agentic settings, where trajectories contain actions, observations, and reasoning that can be transformed into PI in many ways.\nL463: GRPO $+$ OPSD uses the advantage $\\lambda A_{i}+(1-\\lambda)A^{\\text{OPSD}}_{i,t}$, where $A^{\\text{OPSD}}_{i,t}=\\ell_{T}-\\ell_{S}$ is the per-token OPSD advantage, with $\\ell_{S}$ and $\\ell_{T}$ the log-probabilities of the sampled token under the student and the hinted teacher; $\\lambda=1$ recovers GRPO. The GRPO-based runs train for 200 steps on ALFWorld, use learning rate $3\\times 10^{-6}$ on AppWorld, and train on a 424-task pool for 200 steps on $\\tau^{2}$-retail.\nL464: ## Appendix C Offline Metric Protocols\nL465: \nL466: This appendix describes how each offline metric of Section cite12†2.2 is computed; the resulting values are reported in Table cite147†4 . We compute the metrics on 200 randomly sampled ALFTrain tasks, the AppWorld train and dev splits, and 132 matched $\\tau^{2}$-retail training tasks. These tasks are disjoint from the evaluation sets used for post-training performance.\nL467: ### C.1 PI Utility\nL468: \nL469: We evaluate each student with and without the PI under the evaluation settings of Appendix cite58†B.3 , sampling four rollouts per task in each condition; with the PI, the hint is inserted into the model’s own prompt through the teacher template of Appendix cite84†H.1 . The utility of a PI (Equation cite127†2 ) is the difference in performance between the two conditions, paired per task. Figure cite113†3 and Table cite147†4 use $\\Delta$pass@1 for all three benchmarks.\nL470: ### C.2 Sampled-token Reverse KL\nL471: On a subset of tasks from each benchmark, we sample rollouts from each student without PI: 128 per task on ALFWorld and AppWorld, and 32 per task on $\\tau^{2}$-retail. Each rollout is then scored by the same model conditioned on the PI, which gives the teacher’s log-probability of every sampled token. We compute the absolute log-ratio of Equation cite148†3 and average it over all rollouts and tasks to obtain $D$. Every PI scores the same rollouts, so differences in $D$ reflect only the PI.\nL472: ### C.3 Error-step KL Contrast\nL473: Following HINT-SD [cite104†33 ], we label error steps with GPT-5.6 Sol at medium reasoning effort, using the prompts below. The trajectory is serialized as numbered steps, each with the agent’s reasoning (truncated to its first 900 and last 400 characters), its action and the observation that followed, and the judge marks up to two error steps.\nL474: On 32 rollouts per task, we then compute $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ over the flagged steps of failed rollouts (Equations cite149†4 –cite150†6 ), subtracting the task- and position-matched baseline of Equation cite151†5 . The labels depend only on the rollout, so every PI is evaluated on the same steps.\nL475: Error-step labeling, system prompt ({domain} is “ALFWorld household-task”, “AppWorld tool-use” or “tau2-bench retail customer-service”).\nL476: \nL477: You analyze failed {domain} trajectories. Identify up to 2 problematic steps where the agent made mistakes (between 1 and 2 steps total, ordered earliest first). For EACH problematic step, write a distinct correction in less than three sentences that targets only that step's mistake. Output valid JSON only, no other text.\nL478: Error-step labeling, user prompt ({benchmark} is “ALFWorld”, “AppWorld” or “tau2 retail”).\nL479: \nL480: Review this failed {benchmark} attempt and identify the problematic steps.\nL481: Return one (step, feedback) pair per problematic step.\nL482: {trajectory}\nL483: Output format (JSON only, no other text):\nL484: {\"failures\": [{\"step\": <1-indexed step number>, \"feedback\": \"<correction for this step>\"}, ...]}\nL485: ## Appendix D How Information Leakage Distorts PI Utility\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPredicting Privileged Information Effectiveness for On-Policy Self-Distillation (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1)\nciteturn41276view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1\",\"pattern\":\"5 Controlled\"}); Total lines: 1041\nNo matching text found for \"5 Controlled\""
